@packages/lsp import { SigilLsp }
@packages/cli import { SigilCli }
@packages/compiler import { SigilCompiler }

component SigilVsCodeExtension {
  goal {
    Provide a VS Code authoring and review surface for file-backed Sigil
    workspaces.

    Connect editor-native features to the bundled language server without
    duplicating deterministic Sigil semantics.
  }

  interface {
    ExtensionPackage {
      provides an installable VSIX whose artifact version is owned by package.json

      the VSIX is distributed through a versioned GitHub Release
    }

    EditorLanguageSupport {
      registers the sigil language for .sigil files

      provides TextMate syntax highlighting before extension-host activation

      starts the bundled language server through vscode-languageclient

      surfaces diagnostics, document symbols, definition navigation, hover, and
      semantic highlighting

      accepts the bundled server's dynamic registration for supported ownership
      implementation-source changes
    }

    ComponentPreview {
      contributes the Sigil: Show Component Preview command

      opens the selected component contract and collected expansions as read-only
      Markdown
    }

    ConfigurationSchema {
      contributes JSON Schema validation and completion for .sigil/config.json using
      the bundled Sigil configuration schema
    }

    SupportedExtensionHosts {
      runs as a workspace extension in trusted desktop and remote Node extension
      hosts

      supports file-backed workspaces and file URIs

      does not support VS Code for the Web or virtual workspaces
    }

    ReadOnlyEditorSupport {
      collects no telemetry

      does not modify Sigil documents
    }

    CompilationSurface {
      contributes the Sigil: Compile Component and Sigil: Compile Workspace commands

      provides a clickable Sigil Compilation status item for selecting design
      readiness or implementation alignment

      invokes a compatible external sigil compile process instead of bundling
      SigilCompiler

      displays focus-aware compilation progress, red, yellow, or green status, and
      source-ranged findings from the authoritative CompilationReport

      groups findings by component, stage, severity, and lifecycle
    }
  }
}

expand SigilVsCodeExtension {
  state {
    EditorLanguageSupport {
      The language client lifecycle is stopped, starting, running, or stopping.
    }

    ComponentPreview {
      Preview selection reflects the active Sigil editor and cursor when the command
      runs.
    }

    CompilationSurface {
      The active compilation is idle, starting, running, cancelling, completed,
      failed, or superseded.

      The displayed report is current or stale relative to the active document and
      selected profile and focus.

      The configured compilation focus is ask, design, or implementation.

      The displayed focus is design or implementation when a focused result exists.
    }
  }

  logic {
    EditorLanguageSupport {
      Opening or activating a Sigil document starts the bundled server when it is not
      already running.

      The language client forwards document lifecycle and language-feature requests
      through LSP.

      The language client delivers file events for watcher patterns dynamically
      registered by the bundled server.

      Startup and runtime failures are written to the Sigil output channel and
      surfaced to the user.

      Deactivation stops the language client and server process.
    }

    ComponentPreview {
      The preview command requests the standard hover result at the active cursor.

      A successful result opens read-only Markdown beside the Sigil document.

      A missing component or unavailable hover result shows a non-destructive
      informational message.
    }

    ConfigurationSchema {
      The extension maps .sigil/config.json to the bundled schema through VS Code's
      standard jsonValidation contribution, and VS Code performs completion and
      validation.
    }

    CompilationSurface {
      Compile Component passes the active Sigil file and cursor position to external
      sigil compile for compiler-owned component resolution.

      Compile Workspace invokes external sigil compile for the configured workspace.

      ask presents design readiness and implementation alignment before invoking
      compilation.

      design invokes the CLI with --focus design and implementation invokes it with
      --focus implementation.

      Clicking the compilation status item always permits explicit focus selection
      for the active component and provides workspace compilation and profile
      configuration actions.

      Component compilation uses the workspace folder containing the active document.

      Workspace compilation requests a folder selection when a multi-root window has
      more than one eligible file-backed workspace folder.

      The extension consumes structured compiler events and projects final findings
      into a dedicated diagnostic collection and compilation view.

      Starting a replacement run for the same target and profile cancels or
      supersedes the obsolete run.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    ArtifactVersionOwnership {
      package.json owns the extension artifact version.

      The extension Sigil contract does not duplicate its manifest-owned version
      literal.
    }

    ExtensionPackage {
      The extension bundles its client and Node-compatible server artifacts.

      Users require neither a globally installed Deno runtime nor a Sigil CLI.

      Distribution uses GitHub-hosted VSIX assets rather than Visual Studio
      Marketplace or Open VSX.
    }

    EditorLanguageSupport {
      The extension uses stable public VS Code APIs and vscode-languageclient.

      The extension depends on SigilLsp for language semantics and does not import or
      duplicate core behavior.

      Component preview uses standard hover and introduces no custom protocol method.

      Ownership file watching is notification-only and does not modify workspace
      files.
    }

    ConfigurationSchema {
      The bundled schema is a synchronized copy of spec/sigil-config.schema.json
      representing the SigilWorkspaceConfig contract without extending it.

      The schema ships inside the VSIX and loads without network access; VS Code's
      standard JSON language service performs validation and duplicates neither the
      Sigil LSP nor sigil check.
    }

    SupportedExtensionHosts {
      extensionKind is workspace.

      Extension activation requires a trusted workspace under the current manifest
      behavior.

      File-backed desktop and remote Node hosts are supported; web and virtual hosts
      are not.
    }

    ReadOnlyEditorSupport {
      The extension registers no document-editing, file-mutation, or telemetry
      behavior.
    }

    CompilationSurface {
      Agentic compilation is explicit and does not run automatically while typing.

      Deterministic LSP diagnostics remain live independently from agentic
      compilation.

      Focus selection is invocation-level editor control and does not create Git
      metadata, implementation attestations, or persistent workflow state.

      The VSIX does not bundle SigilCompiler or require it for ordinary language
      support.

      Compilation commands require a compatible external sigil executable and show
      actionable setup guidance when it is missing or incompatible.

      The extension does not reinterpret compiler findings or compute compilation
      color independently.

      The extension labels status with the focus it invoked and displays only
      findings returned by that focus's authoritative report.

      Design focus does not invoke or display current-code-compatibility findings.

      The extension invokes JSON Lines mode, validates protocol compatibility and
      event ordering, and accepts the final CompilationReport only from a completed
      terminal event.

      Every accepted event belongs to the started run, follows a valid lifecycle
      transition, and precedes at most one terminal event.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    EditorLanguageSupport {
      Decision: Bundle the Node-compatible Sigil language server and use standard LSP
      features, including hover-backed component preview, without custom protocol
      methods.

      Scope: Governs the VS Code client-server integration and component preview;
      excludes Sigil language interpretation.

      Trade-offs: Bundling increases package size and requires rebuilding the server
      with the extension.

      Consequences: Preview, navigation, diagnostics, and highlighting remain
      compatible with the shared LSP contract.

      Revisit when: A separately installed shared server becomes operationally
      preferable.
    }

    ConfigurationSchema {
      Decision: Provide .sigil/config.json authoring support by bundling the
      published spec/sigil-config.schema.json and associating it through VS Code's
      standard jsonValidation contribution, rather than adding configuration
      validation to the Sigil LSP.

      Scope: Governs .sigil/config.json editor authoring support; excludes the Sigil
      language, the configuration format, the CLI, and LSP behavior.

      Design issues addressed: Contributors editing configuration get no
      schema-backed help, while the contract already exists as a published schema;
      duplicating it in the LSP would fork the contract.

      Consequences: The schema is a synchronized copy shipped in the VSIX and loaded
      offline; VS Code performs completion and validation; a stale copy is caught by
      a synchronization test and refreshed at build time.

      Revisit when: Configuration authoring needs editor behavior beyond standard
      JSON validation.
    }

    OwnershipCacheInvalidation {
      Decision: Let the bundled language server dynamically register supported
      ownership-source watcher patterns through standard Language Server Protocol
      capability registration.

      Scope: Governs VS Code delivery of registered ownership-source changes;
      excludes ownership parsing, cache storage, and source-support policy.

      Trade-offs: Server-directed registration removes the redundant extension-owned
      watcher while depending on the language client's dynamic-registration support.

      Consequences: The extension does not duplicate supported source patterns and
      the server receives standard workspace/didChangeWatchedFiles notifications.

      Revisit when: The language client cannot reliably honor dynamic registration or
      the protocol provides incremental ownership-index updates.
    }

    SupportedExtensionHosts {
      Decision: Limit the initial extension to trusted file-backed desktop and remote
      Node workspace hosts.

      Scope: Governs the pre-production extension-host support contract; excludes
      future browser and virtual-workspace adapters.

      Trade-offs: Restricted, virtual, and browser workspaces receive no full
      extension support.

      Consequences: The manifest explicitly declares workspace placement and
      unsupported trust or virtual-workspace modes.

      Revisit when: The server supports browser or VS Code filesystem abstractions,
      or a security review approves a useful Restricted Mode subset.
    }

    ExternalCompilerIntegration {
      Decision: Invoke the external sigil compile process and consume its structured
      protocol instead of bundling SigilCompiler into the VSIX.

      Scope: Governs the VS Code compilation surface; ordinary bundled language
      support remains independent.

      Trade-offs: Agentic compilation requires a compatible host-installed Sigil CLI.

      Design issues addressed: Bundling the compiler would increase an extension
      artifact already dominated by its client bundle.

      Consequences: The VSIX remains small and terminal and editor compilation use
      the same runtime.
    }

    LocationBasedCompilation {
      Decision: Pass the active file and cursor position to the external compiler and
      let deterministic Sigil semantics resolve the selected component.

      Scope: Governs VS Code Compile Component targeting and multi-root folder
      selection; excludes workspace-wide compilation targets.

      Trade-offs: Compile Component requires a compatible CLI that supports location
      targets.

      Design issues addressed: Textual prefix scanning cannot resolve expand-only
      files, duplicate component names, or cursor containment reliably.

      Discarded alternatives: Duplicating the Sigil parser in the extension and
      sending only a component name were rejected.

      Consequences: Editor compilation uses one component identity resolved by the
      compiler while the extension remains a thin host facade.
    }

    ExplicitAgenticCompilation {
      Decision: Keep deterministic diagnostics live while running agentic compilation
      only through explicit user commands.

      Scope: Governs VS Code compilation triggering; excludes CI and direct CLI
      invocation.

      Trade-offs: Designers initiate recompilation manually after changing Sigil.

      Consequences: Editing remains responsive and does not incur unexpected agent
      cost or latency.
    }

    UserSelectedCompilationFocus {
      Decision: Make the clickable compilation status item and compile commands offer
      design readiness or implementation alignment, with ask as the default
      configured focus.

      Scope: Governs VS Code focus selection, CLI invocation, and focus-aware status
      presentation; excludes compiler stage semantics, Git integration, and workflow
      lifecycle.

      Trade-offs: ask adds one selection step while explicit default settings remain
      available for frequent use.

      Design issues addressed: One unlabeled red, yellow, or green status conflates
      desired-contract quality with current implementation alignment.

      Discarded alternatives: Inferring focus from repository drift, storing
      implementation completion, and computing editor-owned status were rejected.

      Consequences: Users control the evaluated question from the status bar while
      compiler color and findings remain authoritative.
    }
  }

  cases {
    EditorLanguageSupport {
      Opening a .sigil file applies TextMate highlighting and starts language
      features.

      Malformed source displays ranged diagnostics.

      Imported components and concepts support navigation, hover, and semantic
      highlighting.

      Changing an ownership annotation invalidates the server cache and the next
      component or concept hover shows refreshed links.

      A failed bundled server produces visible output and an error without crashing
      VS Code.

      Deactivation stops the client and server.
    }

    ComponentPreview {
      Running preview on a component opens read-only Markdown.

      Running preview without an active component leaves editors unchanged and shows
      information.
    }

    ConfigurationSchema {
      Opening a valid .sigil/config.json provides schema-backed completion without
      diagnostics.

      An unknown configuration property or invalid value shape is reported by VS Code
      JSON validation.
    }

    SupportedExtensionHosts {
      A trusted desktop or remote file-backed workspace receives full language
      support.

      Restricted Mode disables the extension under current manifest defaults.

      A virtual or web workspace receives no supported extension-host contract.
    }

    ExtensionPackage {
      A user installs the VSIX downloaded from GitHub Releases through Install from
      VSIX.
    }

    CompilationSurface {
      Selecting design readiness for the component under the cursor invokes design
      focus and labels progress and the final status as Design.

      Selecting implementation alignment invokes implementation focus and labels
      progress and the final status as Implementation.

      Cancelling ask leaves compilation and displayed findings unchanged.

      A configured design or implementation focus lets component and workspace
      commands run without asking while the clickable status item still permits an
      explicit choice.

      Compiling from an expand-only file evaluates the component represented by that
      expand.

      Compiling a document in the second folder of a multi-root window uses that
      document's workspace folder.

      A missing or incompatible sigil executable leaves ordinary language features
      active and presents setup guidance.

      Recompiling after a correction marks resolved findings through the compiler
      report comparison.

      Editing after a completed run marks its focus-labeled displayed report stale.

      Design focus never displays a current-code-compatibility finding because that
      stage is outside its requested closure.
    }
  }
}
